          "body": "Reusing a global or sticky RegExp in the CORS origin array makes permission depend on its `lastIndex`. The same allowed origin can alternate between accepted and rejected requests, and checking an origin mutates the caller's regex state.\n\nFor these stateful regexes, check from index zero and restore the previous index in `finally`. Ordinary regexes keep their existing path. Regression cases cover both flags, repeated accepted origins, rejected origins and preservation of a pre-existing index.\n\nValidation on Node 24.21.0:\n- Both new regression cases fail before the fix; all 52 CORS unit tests pass after it.\n- Lint, formatting, typecheck and `pnpm build` pass.\n- The full unmodified test fixture run reports only the existing loopback IP expectation failure (`::ffff:127.0.0.1`); 3,037 tests pass, 51 skip after `pnpm build` (matching the CI build/test order). Applying the independent loopback test expectation locally allows the full suite to pass (3,038 passed, 51 skipped); that test-only change is excluded from this PR.\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n  * Fixed inconsistent CORS origin checks when matching rules use global or sticky regular expressions. Repeated checks now reliably allow matching origins and reject nonmatching ones, without changing the rule’s existing state.\n* **Tests**\n  * Added regression coverage for repeated checks with global and sticky matching rules, including verification that their prior state is preserved.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->",
