          "pull_request": {
            "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/h3js/h3/pulls/1582",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/h3js/h3/pull/1582",
            "diff_url": "https://github.com/h3js/h3/pull/1582.diff",
            "patch_url": "https://github.com/h3js/h3/pull/1582.patch",
            "merged_at": "2026-10-17T11:57:04Z"
          },
          "body": "when a query value has one malformed escape, `decodeURIComponent` throws and `parseQuery` keeps the whole value undecoded, so the valid escapes stay encoded too. a stray `%` is enough:\n\n```ts\napp.get(\"/search\", (event) => ({\n  getQuery: getQuery(event).q,\n  searchParams: event.url.searchParams.get(\"q\"),\n}));\n// GET /search?q=caf%C3%A9+50%\n// main: {\"getQuery\":\"caf%C3%A9 50%\",\"searchParams\":\"café 50%\"}\n// this: {\"getQuery\":\"café 50%\",\"searchParams\":\"café 50%\"}\n```\n\n`URLSearchParams` and `node:querystring` both decode the valid escapes and keep the malformed ones as-is. now the catch falls back to that instead of skipping, for keys and values. the happy path doesn't change.\n\nthe test compares `parseQuery` with `URLSearchParams` on a few malformed inputs, it fails on main.\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n  * Query strings with malformed percent-encoded keys or values are now decoded more consistently, including when valid and malformed escapes appear together.\n  * Malformed escapes are decoded only once, and query parsing better matches browser behavior for valid and invalid escape sequences. Malformed `__proto__` keys are also preserved as query properties without changing the parsed object’s prototype.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->",
